WEBVTT

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.608
Interoperability covers three memory workflows: WasmGPU's own

00:00:07.708 --> 00:00:10.907
driver memory, arenas inside it, and data

00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:14.689
owned by foreign WebAssembly modules or Python.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.546
WasmGPU.driver exposes the Rust driver's own linear memory,

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:25.737
with typed views and heap or frame-scoped slices.

00:00:26.166 --> 00:00:31.003
Memory can grow and replace its buffer, so typed views must be reacquired

00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:35.197
after growth, while slices hand out a fresh view on each call.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.897
Shared-memory checks report whether that memory is a

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:42.817
SharedArrayBuffer, which the default build does not require.

00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:47.212
The frame arena is a bump allocator for

00:00:47.312 --> 00:00:50.585
per-frame scratch data, eight mebibytes by default.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.412
Each allocation advances a head pointer, aligned to sixteen bytes

00:00:55.512 --> 00:00:59.646
by default, and usedBytes and capBytes report the fill level.

00:01:00.066 --> 00:01:05.672
Reset rewinds the head and advances the epoch, which invalidates every

00:01:05.772 --> 00:01:10.889
frame slice; WasmGPU.run calls reset at the start of each frame.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.616
createHeapArena reserves one block from the

00:01:15.716 --> 00:01:18.737
WebAssembly heap and bump-allocates inside it.

00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:22.834
Its slices expire when the arena resets or is destroyed,

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:26.064
and destroy returns the whole block to the heap.

00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:29.422
Heap slices from the driver are different: each

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.764
stays valid until its own free call.

00:01:33.666 --> 00:01:39.097
WasmGPU.webassembly wraps another module's instance, exports,

00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:41.545
or memory, as a WasmModule.

00:01:41.966 --> 00:01:46.333
Views over that memory take a pointer, a length, and a dtype; the

00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:50.869
range is validated, and the pointer must be aligned for the dtype.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.696
Pointers and lengths can come from exported functions or globals,

00:01:55.796 --> 00:02:00.330
and refresh re-resolves them after the module reallocates or grows.

00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:04.797
Geometry and the scientific objects accept these views as

00:02:04.897 --> 00:02:08.743
borrowed sources, copy them into their own GPU buffers

00:02:08.843 --> 00:02:11.812
on refresh, and never free foreign memory.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:18.857
In Pyodide, the Python interop copies a Python buffer into

00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:24.172
a new CPUndarray, or uploads it straight into a GPUndarray.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.250
Inputs must be C-contiguous, in one of eight dtypes,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.073
and copyInto requires an exact dtype and shape match.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.624
A buffer the interop acquires is released exactly once, even

00:02:36.724 --> 00:02:40.848
on failure, while a buffer you pass directly stays borrowed.

00:02:41.266 --> 00:02:45.145
On the Python side, a helper class normalizes NumPy

00:02:45.245 --> 00:02:49.358
arrays with never, if-needed, or always copy policies.

00:02:51.266 --> 00:02:55.451
WasmGPU.webgpu builds bind group layout entries for

00:02:55.551 --> 00:02:59.904
storage, uniform, sampler, and texture bindings, with

00:03:00.004 --> 00:03:03.433
compute visibility by default for buffers.

00:03:03.833 --> 00:03:08.640
Its resource helpers normalize storage buffers, raw GPU buffers,

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:13.546
buffer ranges, samplers, and texture views into binding entries.

00:03:13.966 --> 00:03:17.815
It only describes resources: compute pipelines and custom

00:03:17.915 --> 00:03:20.586
materials still create and run the work.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.099
To recap: driver memory views, the frame arena, heap arenas,

00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:32.661
foreign module views, Python transfers, and declarative WebGPU layouts.

00:03:34.066 --> 00:03:35.823
The full reference is in the

00:03:35.923 --> 00:03:38.873
Interoperability section of the documentation.
